Makefile.libretro forwards OVERRIDE_CC/OVERRIDE_CXX to MAME's genie build only
on the Windows and Apple branches.  On Linux nothing is forwarded, so genie
falls back to plain `gcc`/`g++` and the whole core is built with the HOST
compiler -- the cross toolchain in CC/CXX is silently ignored, and the link
then fails (or, worse, produces an x86 object on an ARM target).

Add a generic opt-in: when CROSS_BUILD is set, pass the compilers through.
CROSS_BUILD is genie's own flag for "host tools with the host compiler, target
objects with OVERRIDE_CC", which is exactly the split needed here.  Guarded by
ifneq so a native build is completely unaffected.

Two more values have to travel with them, both derived from the BUILD host
otherwise:

  PLATFORM      makefile:127-172 picks it from `uname -m`, so an x86_64 host
                yields --PLATFORM=x86 and genie generates an x86 build.  MAME's
                own names (arm, arm64) are the same tokens LIBRETRO_CPU uses.
  ARCHITECTURE  makefile:273-337 sets it to _x64 on a 64-bit host, and
                makefile:290-297 turns that into MPARAM := -m64, which an
                aarch64 gcc rejects outright ("unrecognized command-line option
                '-m64'").  Clearing it is exactly what MAME's own ARM branches
                (makefile:355 onwards) do.

--- a/Makefile.libretro
+++ b/Makefile.libretro
@@ -190,6 +190,11 @@
 ifneq ($(LIBRETRO_CPU),)
 	PLATFLAGS += ARCH="" LIBRETRO_CPU="$(LIBRETRO_CPU)"
 endif
+ifneq ($(CROSS_BUILD),)
+	PLATFLAGS += OVERRIDE_CC="$(CC)" OVERRIDE_CXX="$(CXX)"
+	PLATFLAGS += CROSS_BUILD="$(CROSS_BUILD)"
+	PLATFLAGS += PLATFORM="$(LIBRETRO_CPU)" ARCHITECTURE=""
+endif
 ifneq ($(FORCE_DRC_C_BACKEND),)
 	PLATFLAGS += FORCE_DRC_C_BACKEND="$(FORCE_DRC_C_BACKEND)"
 endif
